WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.810
Hello, Conan O'Brien here.

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:06.360
All last week, it was
my morsel of television,

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.000
to hear black voices as we struggle

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.570
with this intense period of
racial anguish and upheaval.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:14.910
I'd like to continue that tonight.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:17.100
Earlier today, I spoke with the mayor

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.770
of St. Paul, Minnesota,
Mayor Melvin Carter.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:24.130
He is at the epicenter
of this national crisis,

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:26.410
and he has a unique perspective.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:29.373
So without any delay, let's get started.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:33.330
While George Floyd may be the most notable

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:34.720
African American to have died

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:36.310
at the hands of Minnesota police,

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:37.960
he was not the first.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.210
In 2018, tonight's guest was elected

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:43.990
the first African American
mayor of St. Paul, Minnesota,

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:47.070
two years after the
Philando Castile shooting

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.830
in the neighboring suburb of St. Anthony.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:53.170
He's a fourth generation
resident of St. Paul,

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:56.380
and the son of a former
St. Paul police officer.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:00.250
I'm very honored to welcome
Mayor Melvin Carter.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:01.520
Mayor, thank you for being here.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.110
I know you're busy, this
means a lot to us, thank you.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.347
Thank you for having
me on, I appreciate it.

00:01:06.347 --> 00:01:09.670
You know, we'll start

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:11.860
for me to just ask you
personally how you're doing,

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:14.490
you, your family, everyone doing okay

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:16.600
during these insane times?

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:19.260
You know that's a super
loaded question these days,

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:21.900
and I think some of it
depends on what you mean

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.686
and some of it depends on what the day is.

00:01:24.686 --> 00:01:26.800
We have a pretty big family.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:30.020
We've got a crew of children
in and out of the house,

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.190
who have been trying
to socially distance--

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:34.880
You know social distance learning at home.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.520
Right, right.
And that's been fun,

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:38.808
as fun as it has been for everybody else.

00:01:38.808 --> 00:01:40.570
We've got a three-month-old at home.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:41.403
Yeah, I want to talk about that.

00:01:41.403 --> 00:01:44.370
You have a three-month-old,
so in the midst of

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.790
this incredible turmoil, not just COVID,

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:50.920
but all of the unrest and anguish

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:54.360
of the past week and a half,
you have a three-month-old.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.330
That's right.
And so, I just

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:58.820
want to say you're welcome
to come stay with me.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:01.400
Yeah, right, I appreciate that.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:03.580
See, try as I might, she doesn't have

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:06.000
the proper appreciation
yet for the term mayor.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.539
I try to convince her that like,

00:02:07.539 --> 00:02:09.060
one day this will mean a lot to you,

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.270
so like, let me get some
sleep, but no, it doesn't work.

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:14.470
You're not getting the respect you should.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:18.390
Well, let me start,
because one of the reasons

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:19.537
I really wanted to talk to you,

00:02:19.537 --> 00:02:21.700
and again, I am so appreciative that you

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.900
could make the time for us, is that

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:28.900
you're kind of, because
you're the mayor of St. Paul,

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:33.270
and also the son of a police officer,

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:36.633
and you are at the nexus of so many,

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:39.897
you know, points right now.
Right.

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:43.900
And your point of view, I
think, is incredibly valuable,

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:46.190
obviously, and so I wanted to start

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:48.570
by talking to you, I
mentioned it up front,

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:52.483
the Philando Castile
shooting several years ago,

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.480
which was another horrific incident

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.780
which was caught on video,
witnessed by a lot of people.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:04.280
You had a personal
connection to that incident.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:08.120
I know it happened in a
neighboring jurisdiction

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.710
just outside St. Paul.
That's right.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:13.400
You knew Philando Castile, is that true?

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.270
Not well, I would see
him around community.

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:17.930
He graduated from my same high school

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:20.420
a year away from my younger sister,

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:22.040
a year ahead of my younger sister.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:23.590
The school he worked at

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:25.990
was the school that my children
went to kindergarten in,

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:27.720
and so he was around community.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.040
I would see him at the coffee shop,

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.890
and I would see him
around community events,

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:32.900
but didn't know him well, but of course,

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:36.010
it's really traumatic
for our entire community

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:37.950
for something like that to play out,

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.840
and while the rest of
the world was kind of

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:44.610
battling with this major
kind of sociopolitical thing,

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:47.810
we were, locally,
explaining to our children

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:48.870
what happened to Mr. Phil,

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:51.170
so it was traumatic on a bunch of levels.

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:54.193
Right, right, it was a
person in your community

00:03:54.193 --> 00:03:57.060
that you knew.
That's right.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:00.971
And now that event,
obviously, very traumatic,

00:04:00.971 --> 00:04:02.760
and got a lot of attention,

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:04.890
but George Floyd seems to, obviously,

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:07.570
has blown this wide open,

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:11.210
and been just

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:13.870
an incredibly wrenching moment

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:16.140
for a lot of Americans,

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:20.245
probably exposing a lot of us to a reality

00:04:20.245 --> 00:04:22.980
that we were not aware of,

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.670
but you grew up in this reality.

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:27.080
Yeah.
You grew up,

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:29.100
your father was on the police force,

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.240
but also you had,

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:35.320
I'm guessing, interactions of your own

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:38.720
with maybe being pulled over, or--

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.230
Definitely.
Treated differently

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:41.820
because of the color of your skin.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:43.240
Yeah, without a doubt.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:45.290
You know, to the video of George Floyd,

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:48.380
I mean, unfortunately
we've had this conversation

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:51.720
for years now, which is how bad

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:53.412
does a video have to be?

00:04:53.412 --> 00:04:55.060
You know, how egregious
does it have to be?

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:57.470
How obvious does it have to be

00:04:57.470 --> 00:04:59.140
for us to say okay, wait, that's enough,

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:01.560
somebody's gotta be held
accountable for this?

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.890
And we said that after
Philando Castile was killed,

00:05:03.890 --> 00:05:05.730
and like, okay, this clearly,

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:07.283
for it to be this obvious, it's clear

00:05:07.283 --> 00:05:09.480
that somebody has to be held accountable,

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:11.470
and then we saw that officer acquitted,

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:14.240
and so it seems that with each video

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:16.110
it keeps sort of testing the limits

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:19.650
of just how much that

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:21.080
somebody can get away with

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:24.980
with regard to taking a
black life or a brown life.

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:27.040
Yes, I've had lots of experiences,

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:29.420
you know, frankly, good and bad, right?

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:31.450
I have the privilege,
it's a level of privilege

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:35.130
to grow up with my dad
being a police officer,

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:37.680
so I had this, my first car was a 1984

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.470
two-tone gray Monte Carlo, right?

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:43.410
It was very sweet in a way
that only 17-year-old boys

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:46.272
can truly appreciate.
Yes, I know what

00:05:46.272 --> 00:05:48.072
you're talking about, yeah.
Right, right.

00:05:48.072 --> 00:05:49.817
And so I'd get pulled
over all the time, right?

00:05:49.817 --> 00:05:51.500
And when I got pulled over,

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:53.170
they'd run my plates and
they'd run to my father,

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:55.460
who was in internal affairs at the time,

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:59.563
and I would get let go
with a warning, right?

00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:05.330
But you're right, part of my truth is

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:07.290
you shouldn't have to be
a police officer's son.

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:09.500
You shouldn't have to be a college student

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:12.530
or a city council member,
or somebody special

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:14.900
to be treated with basic dignity.

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:16.610
I think Van Jones said it really well

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:18.730
in your conversation with him is that

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:22.300
the trauma of the violence

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:25.460
of that George Floyd video is that

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:28.060
it cracks all of the remaining solace

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:29.730
that we take in those conversations

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:31.200
that we have with our children about

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:33.490
if you just behave, if you just

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:35.000
do everything that they say,

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:37.130
if you just don't resist,

00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:39.060
then you'll be able to kinda walk out of

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:40.600
that conversation safely.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:42.250
He did all of those things, and I tell you

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:45.140
maybe the most heartbreaking
part about that video

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:48.430
is the paradox that he didn't even fight

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:49.700
to try to breathe for his--

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:53.370
To try to fight, to struggle
for his last breath,

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:55.990
but we all know that if he had,

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:57.710
that struggle would have been used

00:06:57.710 --> 00:07:00.500
as the excuse to cover up the fact

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:03.230
that he was killed in the way that he was.

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:07.490
Now we're at this national moment today,

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:10.320
very much the conversation
and the conversation

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:14.990
over the weekend, and last
night many demonstrations

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:17.450
have been about the police,

00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:20.720
and I'm sure

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:22.060
you have very strong opinions.

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:24.146
There's people out there that,

00:07:24.146 --> 00:07:26.610
there's a big movement
to defund the police.

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:29.910
Yeah.
And I would imagine

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:33.860
that like many things, it's complicated.

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:36.870
It's probably a very complicated question.

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:38.240
When you hear about defunding the police,

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:39.480
there are many people, including myself,

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:41.780
who don't really know what that means.

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:43.144
Sure, sure.

00:07:43.144 --> 00:07:46.270
I'll tell you what I
think is not complicated

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:49.430
is the fact that we've
bought in to this notion,

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:52.060
this myth, that public safety is just

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:56.420
as simple as police, prisons,
and prosecutors, right?

00:07:56.420 --> 00:07:58.600
And every time we feel uncomfortable,

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:00.870
or every time we feel unsafe as a nation,

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:03.350
we can find billions of
dollars to double down

00:08:03.350 --> 00:08:05.560
on police, prisons, and prosecutors,

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:06.810
yet it hasn't worked.

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:09.430
We still don't feel safe, you know?

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:11.810
And one of the truths I think
we have to grapple with is

00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:13.790
the reason we don't all feel safe, Conan,

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:16.669
is because that public safety framework,

00:08:16.669 --> 00:08:18.900
that model, really isn't designed

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:19.860
to make us all feel safe.

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:22.000
It's designed to draw a circle

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.140
and protect or insulate the
people inside the circle

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:28.010
from the people outside of
the circle at all costs.

00:08:28.010 --> 00:08:30.210
It creates a level of resentment

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:33.010
from the people who feel like
they're outside of the circle.

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:36.800
It definitely creates a
feeling of being threatened

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:38.290
by the people inside the circle,

00:08:38.290 --> 00:08:40.500
'cause it's clearly a tenuous balance,

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:42.260
and then we drop our police officers

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:44.600
right in the epicenter of all of that

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:47.190
in a way that community members

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:50.618
are gonna look at them as you're the force

00:08:50.618 --> 00:08:52.690
that's kind of here to keep me out

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:56.060
of the greater prosperity of
kinda the broader community.

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:57.940
And so, as we talk to our police officers,

00:08:57.940 --> 00:08:59.410
and we hear them say all the time,

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:01.710
we don't have the
capacity or the expertise

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:04.530
to be the social workers,
the mental health counselors,

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:07.000
or the grief counselors,
or the drug counselors,

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:08.210
or all of those different things

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:10.550
that we all know would help in community.

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:13.637
And so, one thing I think is very clear is

00:09:13.637 --> 00:09:16.910
if we just approach public safety

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:19.670
with this police only perspective

00:09:19.670 --> 00:09:21.450
that we have for so long,

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:23.260
to me that's like
approaching public health

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:25.740
with an ambulance only perspective, right?

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:27.360
We know how ridiculous that would be

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:30.060
to just chase problems after it happens.

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:31.620
We have to start investing in families

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:34.640
and in neighborhoods to try
to reduce the likelihood

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:37.050
that something bad is gonna
happen in the first place,

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:39.590
so it starts with, we hear
our police officers saying

00:09:39.590 --> 00:09:41.460
we can't be the mental health counselors

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:43.730
and the social workers
and the drug counselors.

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:45.840
All of those things,
they're taking orders.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:48.080
Like those are things that
we're clearly identifying

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:52.115
a need for, so we ought
to invest in those things.

00:09:52.115 --> 00:09:54.610
We know, Conan, I'm guessing you live

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:56.080
in a safe neighborhood, right?

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:57.467
I do, yeah.

00:09:57.467 --> 00:09:59.920
And I would guess that
in your neighborhood,

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:01.360
you have strong schools.

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:04.440
I would guess that folks have
access to really good jobs.

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:06.850
I would guess that folks have the ability

00:10:06.850 --> 00:10:08.410
to kinda make their money work for them,

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:11.080
and live a quality life,

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:15.560
and so if we're just investing money

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:18.330
to hire people to chase
problems after they happen,

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:21.108
then that's the opposite
of investing to transform,

00:10:21.108 --> 00:10:23.870
our most challenged neighborhoods

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:25.110
into those neighborhoods that have

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:27.010
the kind of social capital
that your neighborhoods have,

00:10:27.010 --> 00:10:28.240
and that's, I think, at the core

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:29.640
of what people are asking for.

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:33.060
It feels to me, 'cause
I'm familiar that you

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:35.470
do want more community involvement,

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:38.620
and it sounds to me, and
it makes a lot of sense

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:41.460
that you're advocating
a more nuanced approach,

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:45.820
meaning it's not just police,
you take care of everything.

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:48.100
It's trying to figure out

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:51.080
what situations should we
not be sending the police

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:53.200
in to handle, but be sending someone else

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:55.360
in to handle, is that correct?

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:57.230
That's exactly right.

00:10:57.230 --> 00:11:01.460
Any gun safety instructor,
or any police officer

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:03.166
would tell you that even if you're

00:11:03.166 --> 00:11:06.650
a good guy with good intentions,
just introducing a gun,

00:11:06.650 --> 00:11:09.110
introducing a firearm into a situation

00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:11.970
inherently makes that
situation dangerous, right?

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:14.708
And so when people have
a mental health crisis,

00:11:14.708 --> 00:11:17.420
there's a number of
reasons people call 911.

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:19.350
Sometimes it's because I'm being robbed

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:20.660
and I need an officer to show up.

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:22.250
Sometimes it's because I'm in crisis.

00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:24.540
Sometimes it's because I just
need help with something,

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:27.560
and we need to figure out
how to bring different levels

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:29.730
of resource to kinda meet those needs.

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:30.870
That's something that we're doing,

00:11:30.870 --> 00:11:33.090
I think very well in St. Paul.

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:37.250
We have a chief, Conan, who
is constantly focused on

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:39.870
what he calls making
deposits in our bank of trust

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:41.280
with our community members.

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:42.540
I hear him ask our officers

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:44.650
after anything happens three questions.

00:11:44.650 --> 00:11:46.190
Were your actions reasonable,

00:11:46.190 --> 00:11:48.500
were they necessary, and
were they done with respect?

00:11:48.500 --> 00:11:51.310
And if you can answer yes to
all three of those questions,

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:52.290
then you're probably gonna have

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:55.030
a relatively quick, short
conversation with our chief.

00:11:55.030 --> 00:11:57.460
If you can't answer
yes to those questions,

00:11:57.460 --> 00:11:59.500
reasonable, necessary, and respectful,

00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:02.150
then you're gonna have a longer
conversation with our chief.

00:12:02.150 --> 00:12:05.040
We've engaged community
members in the process

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:08.530
of defining what public
safety means for us,

00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:11.350
and what those types of investments are,

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:14.310
and we're actually already
walking down that road.

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:16.356
We have actually already launched

00:12:16.356 --> 00:12:19.430
a public health, mental
health co-responder model

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:22.710
where we have social workers
trained in mental health

00:12:22.710 --> 00:12:24.310
who are literally in the police car

00:12:24.310 --> 00:12:26.600
riding along with police officers

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:29.103
to respond to some of
those issues that we know

00:12:29.103 --> 00:12:32.410
won't be solved by just showing
up and arresting somebody,

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:34.550
but needs a longer term approach.

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:36.500
We're doing that work and
we want to do more of it,

00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:38.570
and I think it's important to
see the rest of the country

00:12:38.570 --> 00:12:39.990
do some of that as well.

00:12:39.990 --> 00:12:41.830
It's hard for me,
because of what you said,

00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:44.640
not to remember that

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:48.220
the reason Philando Castile was killed,

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:50.330
unnecessarily, shockingly killed,

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:53.660
was that all he did was tell the officer

00:12:54.610 --> 00:12:56.890
I do have a weapon in the car

00:12:56.890 --> 00:12:59.410
and I'm licensed to carry,
and he was saying that

00:12:59.410 --> 00:13:03.130
to create more safety
and to be responsible.

00:13:03.130 --> 00:13:06.963
Tragically, the officer
said you're holding a gun.

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:10.820
Don't bring out the gun, and he wasn't,

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:13.924
and didn't, but ended up getting shot,

00:13:13.924 --> 00:13:17.040
that introducing police

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:19.060
and people with weapons

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:22.220
into every situation obviously

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:24.470
can make that situation instantaneously

00:13:24.470 --> 00:13:27.150
a thousand times more
dangerous and volatile,

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:30.960
and so the idea that you could have

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:33.530
different types of people responding

00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:36.950
in different levels of need

00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:39.140
makes a lot of sense.

00:13:39.140 --> 00:13:42.110
I know that this leads into an initiative

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:46.350
called Eight Can't Wait, and it has--

00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:48.160
There's so much of it, I read through

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:51.210
the Eight Can't Wait program,

00:13:51.210 --> 00:13:52.790
and obviously there's things in there

00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:55.890
that say well, listen, no
more use of choke holds,

00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:58.720
no more clamping down on an airway

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:02.630
or the carotid because
that is so dangerous

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:04.813
and can go so wrong so quickly,

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:08.300
but then there's so many
other initiatives in there

00:14:08.300 --> 00:14:10.850
that once I read them
made intuitive sense,

00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:13.003
which was about creating distance,

00:14:15.228 --> 00:14:17.290
creating a bunch of parameters

00:14:17.290 --> 00:14:21.510
that would make the situation much safer.

00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:23.270
Can you talk about some of those

00:14:23.270 --> 00:14:24.960
initiatives from Eight Can't Wait?

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:26.950
Yeah, without a doubt.

00:14:26.950 --> 00:14:28.140
The first thing you said, Conan,

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:29.653
I think brought together
a number of the things

00:14:29.653 --> 00:14:31.440
that we're talking about.

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:35.520
In that, Philando Castile
just being himself,

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:38.280
and having a firearm, even
though it was in his pocket

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:41.160
and put away and he's
talking about it directly

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:44.640
to let the officer know that it was there

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:49.280
out of courtesy, that just
being himself was a threat.

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:50.950
That officer considered him a threat

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:52.780
just sitting there being himself,

00:14:52.780 --> 00:14:54.520
and therein lies, I think,

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:56.520
a big part of the problem is.

00:14:56.520 --> 00:14:59.590
If the standard by which we determine

00:14:59.590 --> 00:15:01.810
whether somebody can be held accountable

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:05.360
for taking a life is whether
you feel threatened or not,

00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:09.580
and if my very existence, if my oxygen

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:13.330
makes you feel threatened,
well then it's easy to see

00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:15.830
why people all across this
country say wait a minute.

00:15:15.830 --> 00:15:18.650
We have to assert the value of black lives

00:15:18.650 --> 00:15:19.920
in a different way.

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:21.520
That's really critical.

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:24.000
My father was a St. Paul police
officer, you mentioned that.

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:25.860
He served almost three decades,

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:27.950
and I would see him just
go through our community

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:31.210
solving problems and
identifying problems that

00:15:31.210 --> 00:15:32.240
if you're not from our neighborhood,

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:33.630
you probably wouldn't even know

00:15:33.630 --> 00:15:36.230
was a problem in the first place,

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:38.390
and so he was able to serve
our community in ways that

00:15:38.390 --> 00:15:40.360
other officers just could not,

00:15:40.360 --> 00:15:42.300
that nobody else really could

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:44.300
because he's from here,
because he grew up here.

00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:45.390
He was raising his children here.

00:15:45.390 --> 00:15:48.160
He goes to the grocery store,
he goes to church here,

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:51.970
and he's interwoven as
a part of the community.

00:15:51.970 --> 00:15:55.000
And so we have, and I
always hear my father say,

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:58.040
he goes when you're that
much a part of the community

00:15:58.040 --> 00:16:00.250
and when somebody's kids go
to school with your kids,

00:16:00.250 --> 00:16:01.810
or you grew up with their parents,

00:16:01.810 --> 00:16:04.600
or you're that interwoven together,

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:08.310
instead of looking for justifiable reasons

00:16:08.310 --> 00:16:11.050
to use deadly force, you
can find an unlimited

00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:13.410
number of reasons not to use deadly force,

00:16:13.410 --> 00:16:15.920
not to shoot somebody,
not to kill somebody.

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:19.350
So we've signed up for this
Eight Can't Wait campaign,

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:23.430
and it's about rewriting
our use of force policies.

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:25.950
The truth is we did that when
we first came into office.

00:16:25.950 --> 00:16:27.960
Unfortunately, I think the saddest thing

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:29.620
about George Floyd's murder

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:32.280
is how historically unsurprising it is.

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:35.390
We've been on this road
for quite some time,

00:16:35.390 --> 00:16:37.680
and we are where we've gotten

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:41.500
as a result of kinda the
road that we've been on.

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:43.980
And so this was a priority of mine

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:45.450
when we first came into office.

00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:48.240
I think in the first two
or three months in office

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:52.060
we had a two-month
public engagement process

00:16:52.060 --> 00:16:53.810
to engage our community in

00:16:53.810 --> 00:16:55.900
what our use of force policy should be.

00:16:55.900 --> 00:16:57.242
I don't know of--

00:16:57.242 --> 00:17:00.590
I don't know of any police department,

00:17:00.590 --> 00:17:03.620
any agency on the planet
before us, frankly,

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:05.970
that has engaged community members to say

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:08.060
let's come up with an agreement.

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:09.890
What's our compact on what

00:17:09.890 --> 00:17:12.558
our use of force policies should be

00:17:12.558 --> 00:17:14.610
for our police officers?

00:17:14.610 --> 00:17:16.960
The final policy that
we passed two years ago

00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:20.090
includes dozens, I think a couple dozen

00:17:20.090 --> 00:17:23.107
of suggestions that we got
straight from community members

00:17:23.107 --> 00:17:24.880
and I think that's really important.

00:17:24.880 --> 00:17:27.440
So the truth is with that
Eight Can't Wait campaign,

00:17:27.440 --> 00:17:28.870
most of those things are things

00:17:28.870 --> 00:17:31.560
that we actually already
implemented two years ago.

00:17:31.560 --> 00:17:34.970
We wanted to make sure that
there was an explicit--

00:17:34.970 --> 00:17:37.740
You know, that we explicitly addressed

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:40.770
two of the items that the
campaign kinda identified

00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:42.840
as not specifically
addressed in our policy,

00:17:42.840 --> 00:17:45.600
so our chief did a fantastic job

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:46.780
and got that turned around,

00:17:46.780 --> 00:17:49.400
I think within 24 hours of realizing

00:17:49.400 --> 00:17:53.080
that they had alerted
us to this challenge.

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:57.570
But in the end, our policy,
which I think is very important,

00:17:57.570 --> 00:17:58.770
it does a number of things.

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:03.770
It establishes that duty
to deescalate a situation.

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:05.970
It establishes the duty to intervene

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:08.510
that we've all talked
about and seen on display

00:18:08.510 --> 00:18:10.890
as necessary in the past week.

00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:14.870
It changes from where we always
talk about reasonable force.

00:18:14.870 --> 00:18:18.040
It directs our officers to utilize

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:20.460
the minimum amount of force necessary,

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:22.200
the minimal amount of force,

00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:24.750
both reasonable and necessary,
so not just reasonable.

00:18:24.750 --> 00:18:28.460
The question is was it
necessary in that situation?

00:18:28.460 --> 00:18:30.130
And I think pretty intriguingly,

00:18:30.130 --> 00:18:33.840
it distinguishes between
how officers respond

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:35.980
to people who are

00:18:35.980 --> 00:18:39.330
being sort of passively resistant,

00:18:39.330 --> 00:18:41.850
just saying I'm not gonna
get up when you say get up,

00:18:41.850 --> 00:18:44.480
versus people who are being
aggressive toward an officer

00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:46.150
or toward another human being.

00:18:46.150 --> 00:18:47.330
The way we respond to them

00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:49.030
has to be fundamentally different.

00:18:49.910 --> 00:18:52.460
It feels to me that there's
a lot of this, sadly,

00:18:52.460 --> 00:18:57.190
that is, it's kind of common sense.

00:18:57.190 --> 00:18:58.023
It is, yeah.

00:18:58.023 --> 00:19:00.280
You and I are both human beings.

00:19:00.280 --> 00:19:03.330
I've been in the world
a lot longer than you,

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:06.590
but I know just being on this planet

00:19:06.590 --> 00:19:10.630
that when you treat someone

00:19:10.630 --> 00:19:12.513
like an other,

00:19:13.720 --> 00:19:16.750
you are going to get
that same feeling back.

00:19:16.750 --> 00:19:21.410
When you get to know people,
when you talk to them,

00:19:21.410 --> 00:19:23.470
when you ask them how you're doing

00:19:23.470 --> 00:19:25.810
and you show any amount of empathy,

00:19:25.810 --> 00:19:29.630
you are creating a
contract, a social contract,

00:19:29.630 --> 00:19:32.380
and these are things that we all know,

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:36.680
we all know, but I think
it's something in our wiring.

00:19:36.680 --> 00:19:39.490
We can forget it or we
can drift away from it,

00:19:39.490 --> 00:19:43.680
so obviously when an officer

00:19:43.680 --> 00:19:45.990
is being sent into a situation

00:19:45.990 --> 00:19:48.636
where for whatever reason
he feels threatened

00:19:48.636 --> 00:19:50.594
or is tense or something,

00:19:50.594 --> 00:19:52.780
he might flip over, there's that circuit

00:19:52.780 --> 00:19:55.260
that flips over into
this person is the other.

00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:56.500
That's right.
Rather than seeing it

00:19:56.500 --> 00:20:00.620
as this is someone I may know,

00:20:00.620 --> 00:20:03.590
someone from my community who
I have had experience with

00:20:03.590 --> 00:20:05.803
because I've been part of the community.

00:20:05.803 --> 00:20:10.230
I think that's where the notion

00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:12.360
that you've been talking about to use

00:20:13.510 --> 00:20:15.460
community policing more,

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:17.750
and I want to use that word carefully

00:20:17.750 --> 00:20:20.700
because obviously police
officers have to be

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:23.170
highly, highly trained, but

00:20:24.090 --> 00:20:27.050
make sure that the community is involved

00:20:27.050 --> 00:20:29.270
in keeping their community safe,

00:20:29.270 --> 00:20:33.400
and that they have some of the power

00:20:33.400 --> 00:20:34.823
and experience to do that.

00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:38.600
I think there's no other way around that.

00:20:38.600 --> 00:20:40.910
My father wrote a book about growing up,

00:20:40.910 --> 00:20:43.624
and I don't want to
spoil it for you, Conan,

00:20:43.624 --> 00:20:47.700
'cause I'm sure you've
got it on your list, but--

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:48.750
I will have it on my list.

00:20:48.750 --> 00:20:50.610
Yeah, I will.
That's right, that's right.

00:20:50.610 --> 00:20:52.340
There's a part of it where he talks about

00:20:52.340 --> 00:20:55.070
when he was 25 and two
of his closest cousins

00:20:55.070 --> 00:20:57.610
and best friends in life were murdered

00:20:57.610 --> 00:21:00.750
in a double homicide, and
they were his closest friends.

00:21:00.750 --> 00:21:02.890
They had grown up together
and somewhere along the way

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:04.720
they had taken a pact
that if somebody kills you

00:21:04.720 --> 00:21:06.010
I'll kill them, right?

00:21:06.010 --> 00:21:08.320
And it's kind of this situation where

00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:11.070
the whole neighborhood assumes

00:21:11.070 --> 00:21:13.400
my father's gonna go after this guy,

00:21:13.400 --> 00:21:15.920
and he ends up getting
acquitted for the killing

00:21:15.920 --> 00:21:18.740
and disappears right after it,

00:21:18.740 --> 00:21:21.840
and my father found out years later,

00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:23.650
probably decades later,

00:21:23.650 --> 00:21:28.260
that my grandfather had
approached the suspect's mother

00:21:28.260 --> 00:21:30.460
and had paid for him
to have a train ticket

00:21:30.460 --> 00:21:32.190
or a bus ticket out of town,

00:21:32.190 --> 00:21:34.120
and that's an example, I think,

00:21:34.120 --> 00:21:35.880
of community working together, right,

00:21:35.880 --> 00:21:37.910
of community identifying a problem,

00:21:37.910 --> 00:21:39.726
community kinda stepping in and saying

00:21:39.726 --> 00:21:41.830
this could go wrong in a way

00:21:41.830 --> 00:21:44.268
that's bad for all of us,

00:21:44.268 --> 00:21:47.150
and let's work together to create

00:21:47.150 --> 00:21:48.630
community level solutions,

00:21:48.630 --> 00:21:50.970
so that's one of the things
that we're investing in as well,

00:21:50.970 --> 00:21:55.338
and there's a number of
communities around the country

00:21:55.338 --> 00:21:57.770
and around the world
who are having success

00:21:57.770 --> 00:22:00.780
with hiring community
members, hiring neighbors

00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:03.370
as interveners and interrupters

00:22:03.370 --> 00:22:06.170
to identify these kind of things

00:22:06.170 --> 00:22:08.090
that could go really bad in the community

00:22:08.090 --> 00:22:10.770
and figure out how, based
on community resources,

00:22:10.770 --> 00:22:13.420
using community, the
language of community,

00:22:13.420 --> 00:22:15.520
how to interrupt and help prevent violence

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:17.030
from happening in the first place,

00:22:17.030 --> 00:22:19.080
and I think that's the core of what

00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:20.640
many of us are saying, right?

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:23.440
Instead of this public safety strategy,

00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:26.200
instead of this kind of
framework and toolbox

00:22:26.200 --> 00:22:28.510
that all waits to start

00:22:28.510 --> 00:22:30.537
until after a crime has been committed...

00:22:30.537 --> 00:22:31.520
Right, right.

00:22:31.520 --> 00:22:35.240
How do we invest proactively
in our neighbors?

00:22:35.240 --> 00:22:39.090
How do we invest proactively
in our neighborhood areas?

00:22:39.090 --> 00:22:41.210
How do we invest
proactively in our families

00:22:41.210 --> 00:22:43.190
and our children in such a way

00:22:43.190 --> 00:22:45.020
that makes people less likely

00:22:45.020 --> 00:22:47.570
to have to act out in desperation?

00:22:47.570 --> 00:22:50.348
It's really, it's preventative health.

00:22:50.348 --> 00:22:52.950
That's what it is.
Versus chemotherapy,

00:22:52.950 --> 00:22:57.320
and I think that's I think what

00:22:57.320 --> 00:22:59.510
I'm hoping this movement,

00:22:59.510 --> 00:23:01.270
this turmoil and this anguish,

00:23:01.270 --> 00:23:04.620
and this activism leads to

00:23:04.620 --> 00:23:07.710
is a sense that everybody knows

00:23:07.710 --> 00:23:10.000
you don't want to wait to the stage where

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:11.540
you're getting chemotherapy.

00:23:11.540 --> 00:23:12.719
That's right.
You want to

00:23:12.719 --> 00:23:13.552
take care of yourself and we want to

00:23:13.552 --> 00:23:16.770
take care of our community and
the people in our community

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:18.980
so they all feel invested.
That's right.

00:23:18.980 --> 00:23:21.365
And you know what I would say, Conan,

00:23:21.365 --> 00:23:23.530
is right now we have the language for it

00:23:23.530 --> 00:23:25.430
because as we know well,

00:23:25.430 --> 00:23:28.390
we're in four different crises
at the same time right now.

00:23:28.390 --> 00:23:31.770
We have the pandemic, the
physical health portion of it,

00:23:31.770 --> 00:23:34.350
we have the economic
portion of the pandemic,

00:23:34.350 --> 00:23:38.650
we have the trauma, the
triggered historic trauma

00:23:38.650 --> 00:23:40.720
of the video of George Floyd's murder,

00:23:40.720 --> 00:23:42.940
and now we have the rebuilding

00:23:42.940 --> 00:23:45.380
that so many cities around
the country have to do

00:23:45.380 --> 00:23:47.150
after all the destruction that's happened

00:23:47.150 --> 00:23:49.160
in the last couple of weeks,

00:23:49.160 --> 00:23:51.980
and so we're facing all of
these things at the same time.

00:23:51.980 --> 00:23:54.020
But one of the things
that the COVID-19 pandemic

00:23:54.020 --> 00:23:56.490
has given us is a language
around this, right?

00:23:56.490 --> 00:23:58.380
We've got this pandemic and Conan,

00:23:58.380 --> 00:24:02.090
we've been talking about
violence as a public health,

00:24:02.090 --> 00:24:03.640
you know, every time we have a shooting,

00:24:03.640 --> 00:24:04.870
or every time we have an incident,

00:24:04.870 --> 00:24:07.780
somebody gets on TV and says
this is a public health crisis.

00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:08.877
And I agree with that,

00:24:08.877 --> 00:24:11.950
but we haven't really started
to employ on any large scale

00:24:11.950 --> 00:24:14.575
public health solutions to it yet.

00:24:14.575 --> 00:24:17.660
The pandemic has given us
the language of testing

00:24:17.660 --> 00:24:19.860
so that we know who's impacted, right,

00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:22.230
so that we know who's
impacted by this thing.

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:23.960
It's given us the language of treatment,

00:24:23.960 --> 00:24:26.030
so that once we identify who's impacted,

00:24:26.030 --> 00:24:27.780
we have to do something to figure out

00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:30.510
how to intervene and help them heal

00:24:30.510 --> 00:24:33.180
from this ailment, and it's given us

00:24:33.180 --> 00:24:34.960
the language of contact tracing to say

00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:37.850
who else might you have
touched with this, right?

00:24:37.850 --> 00:24:40.460
And the interesting thing that

00:24:40.460 --> 00:24:42.530
a bunch of epidemiologists
and people smarter than me

00:24:42.530 --> 00:24:47.130
figured out is that violence
acts like an epidemic.

00:24:47.130 --> 00:24:49.360
Violence does act like
a public health crisis

00:24:49.360 --> 00:24:51.250
in that in the same ways we can predict

00:24:51.250 --> 00:24:54.360
a case of the flu by
having been around someone

00:24:54.360 --> 00:24:55.550
with a case of the flu,

00:24:55.550 --> 00:24:57.780
the best predictor of
an incident of violence

00:24:57.780 --> 00:24:59.700
is a preceding incident of violence,

00:24:59.700 --> 00:25:01.570
and so when we think about employing

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:03.080
exactly what you just described,

00:25:03.080 --> 00:25:06.110
this public health lens to this,

00:25:06.110 --> 00:25:07.670
it creates a whole new--

00:25:07.670 --> 00:25:10.230
It opens a set of doors and
a whole new toolbox for us

00:25:10.230 --> 00:25:13.500
because if we start
identifying that testing

00:25:13.500 --> 00:25:15.697
to say who's been impacted by violence,

00:25:15.697 --> 00:25:18.257
who's most likely to be

00:25:18.257 --> 00:25:21.508
touched by the ailments of
violence in our communities,

00:25:21.508 --> 00:25:24.170
to treat it and say how do we intervene,

00:25:24.170 --> 00:25:26.310
whether that's the mental health,

00:25:26.310 --> 00:25:28.340
whether that's supportive resources,

00:25:28.340 --> 00:25:29.770
whether that's peer to peer counseling,

00:25:29.770 --> 00:25:32.930
how do we intervene to help
kind of break that cycle,

00:25:32.930 --> 00:25:34.570
and who have they been in touch with?

00:25:34.570 --> 00:25:36.798
How do we do the contact tracing

00:25:36.798 --> 00:25:41.199
to help identify, isolate,
and slow this pandemic?

00:25:41.199 --> 00:25:44.140
That to me seems to be
the smartest toolkit.

00:25:44.140 --> 00:25:46.028
It's working in cities around the country.

00:25:46.028 --> 00:25:48.810
That's what we're on the starting lines

00:25:48.810 --> 00:25:50.400
in implementing here in St. Paul,

00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:52.690
and that seems a lot more effective

00:25:52.690 --> 00:25:54.880
and frankly a lot more research based

00:25:54.880 --> 00:25:56.800
than just saying oh, we don't feel safe

00:25:56.800 --> 00:25:58.860
so we're gonna put another

00:25:58.860 --> 00:26:00.830
billions of dollars into the police,

00:26:00.830 --> 00:26:02.750
prosecutors and prisons.

00:26:02.750 --> 00:26:05.830
This week and a half that
we've just experienced

00:26:05.830 --> 00:26:09.280
doesn't just pass away

00:26:09.280 --> 00:26:11.750
as so many things in our culture do.

00:26:11.750 --> 00:26:13.900
We have such a short attention span,

00:26:13.900 --> 00:26:16.470
and we tend to move from crisis to crisis

00:26:16.470 --> 00:26:17.783
or whatever's trending,

00:26:19.067 --> 00:26:22.670
and we don't want this situation

00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:25.120
to be something that trended
for a couple of weeks

00:26:25.120 --> 00:26:28.380
and then went away without
there being lasting change.

00:26:28.380 --> 00:26:30.003
Are you optimistic?

00:26:31.150 --> 00:26:33.600
I would like to be optimistic that,

00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:36.410
because this does feel different.

00:26:36.410 --> 00:26:39.170
I was too young in 1968 to experience

00:26:40.770 --> 00:26:42.230
that upheaval.

00:26:42.230 --> 00:26:45.930
This does feel, in my lifetime, 57 years,

00:26:45.930 --> 00:26:48.460
it's the biggest thing that I've witnessed

00:26:48.460 --> 00:26:50.210
and my hope is that it's lasting.

00:26:50.210 --> 00:26:51.690
Do you feel this could be lasting,

00:26:51.690 --> 00:26:54.290
that if we keep doing the work,

00:26:54.290 --> 00:26:55.660
that people will stay focused

00:26:55.660 --> 00:26:58.330
and this could lead us to a better place?

00:26:58.330 --> 00:27:00.747
I am hopeful about that, and I'll tell you

00:27:00.747 --> 00:27:02.980
one of the amazing
things that I've learned

00:27:02.980 --> 00:27:04.940
is just to bet on the human spirit,

00:27:04.940 --> 00:27:06.720
and that every time something just

00:27:08.030 --> 00:27:10.590
horrifically ugly happens in community

00:27:10.590 --> 00:27:13.190
that we see this beautiful
human spirit show up

00:27:13.190 --> 00:27:15.750
and so in St. Paul and in
many of the other cities

00:27:15.750 --> 00:27:18.680
around the country where we've seen just

00:27:18.680 --> 00:27:21.951
massive, heartbreaking
levels of destruction,

00:27:21.951 --> 00:27:24.489
and Conan, I want to be really clear,

00:27:24.489 --> 00:27:26.900
and I think you've spoken to this already,

00:27:26.900 --> 00:27:28.750
there's sort of two things.

00:27:28.750 --> 00:27:30.250
There were the protestors, right,

00:27:30.250 --> 00:27:32.140
and by protestors I mean the people

00:27:32.140 --> 00:27:36.600
who just are heartbroken
by George Floyd's loss,

00:27:36.600 --> 00:27:39.350
just feel it's unjust and that

00:27:39.350 --> 00:27:41.330
he should still be alive today,

00:27:41.330 --> 00:27:43.050
the people who feel
like these four officers

00:27:43.050 --> 00:27:44.680
must be held accountable in court,

00:27:44.680 --> 00:27:46.570
all four of them, not just the one,

00:27:46.570 --> 00:27:48.920
and feel like we just
have this soul searching,

00:27:48.920 --> 00:27:51.130
deep work to do as a country

00:27:51.130 --> 00:27:54.230
to stop this cycle from
happening over and over again.

00:27:54.230 --> 00:27:57.020
So there's the protestors, but then

00:27:57.020 --> 00:27:59.290
there's also the rioters,
the people who showed up

00:27:59.290 --> 00:28:02.910
to break a window, and to
light a building on fire,

00:28:02.910 --> 00:28:05.600
and to just create
destruction in community.

00:28:05.600 --> 00:28:07.772
And so when I'm talking
about that kind of,

00:28:07.772 --> 00:28:10.450
that violence and destruction that we saw,

00:28:10.450 --> 00:28:13.790
those rioters, who clearly

00:28:13.790 --> 00:28:15.490
could not have been driven

00:28:15.490 --> 00:28:18.170
by just a sense of a need for more justice

00:28:18.170 --> 00:28:20.860
and fairness while stripping our neighbors

00:28:20.860 --> 00:28:22.964
of their ability to make a living

00:28:22.964 --> 00:28:25.160
or our senior citizens of their ability

00:28:25.160 --> 00:28:28.200
to go to the corner and get
their lifesaving medicine

00:28:28.200 --> 00:28:31.270
from their pharmacy in
the middle of a pandemic,

00:28:31.270 --> 00:28:34.120
all that to say is when the
sun came up the next morning,

00:28:34.960 --> 00:28:38.470
it came up on neighbors just
coming out of the woodworks

00:28:38.470 --> 00:28:41.020
with shovels and brooms
and bags and trash cans.

00:28:41.020 --> 00:28:42.270
We were sitting on our block

00:28:42.270 --> 00:28:44.620
and all of a sudden some
music came out of nowhere,

00:28:44.620 --> 00:28:45.897
and we looked up to say
where is that coming from?

00:28:45.897 --> 00:28:48.060
And an army of neighbors
walked up the street

00:28:48.060 --> 00:28:49.010
just cleaning up.

00:28:49.010 --> 00:28:52.160
I mean, it looked like a
scene from the Cat in the Hat,

00:28:52.160 --> 00:28:53.940
like just neighbors
coming out of the woodwork

00:28:53.940 --> 00:28:56.150
to clean up and
volunteering for each other,

00:28:56.150 --> 00:28:57.180
don't even know each other,

00:28:57.180 --> 00:28:58.860
just volunteering for each other.

00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:02.690
We had a business we did a
supply drive last Saturday,

00:29:02.690 --> 00:29:05.240
a business here whose
windows are still boarded up,

00:29:06.090 --> 00:29:09.740
who instead of cleaning up
their own business on Saturday,

00:29:09.740 --> 00:29:11.680
opened up their parking lot

00:29:11.680 --> 00:29:14.240
and were collecting formula and diapers

00:29:14.240 --> 00:29:16.200
and canned foods, and families were coming

00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:18.300
to bring stuff and families
were coming to take stuff,

00:29:18.300 --> 00:29:20.790
and it was just this kind of
massive sense of community.

00:29:20.790 --> 00:29:23.330
I did a walk down one of our main streets

00:29:23.330 --> 00:29:26.000
that was impacted earlier this week,

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:27.450
or I'm sorry, last week,

00:29:27.450 --> 00:29:31.630
and you know, was looking at
businesses who were boarded up,

00:29:31.630 --> 00:29:33.810
and passed two businesses
that were boarded up

00:29:33.810 --> 00:29:35.900
and somebody came out and
said mayor, mayor, mayor.

00:29:35.900 --> 00:29:37.870
And I said yes, and said
I just want you to know

00:29:37.870 --> 00:29:39.640
from two to six p.m. today

00:29:39.640 --> 00:29:42.210
we're feeding community
members free lunch,

00:29:42.210 --> 00:29:44.030
and so if you know anybody who needs food,

00:29:44.030 --> 00:29:46.930
tell them to come by
and get their food here.

00:29:46.930 --> 00:29:49.300
And there are no shortage of those stories

00:29:49.300 --> 00:29:51.360
where people are just
taking care of each other.

00:29:51.360 --> 00:29:53.330
The business owner whose building

00:29:53.330 --> 00:29:54.800
was literally burned to the ground

00:29:54.800 --> 00:29:56.391
and she stopped me to say my business

00:29:56.391 --> 00:29:57.224
was burned to the ground.

00:29:57.224 --> 00:29:58.530
I said I know and I'm really sorry.

00:29:58.530 --> 00:30:00.040
We're gonna help you
rebuild, and she said no,

00:30:00.040 --> 00:30:00.873
that's not what I'm saying.

00:30:00.873 --> 00:30:02.077
I'm saying I have time to help right now.

00:30:02.077 --> 00:30:05.160
I want to help fundraise
for other businesses.

00:30:05.160 --> 00:30:07.310
And I'll tell you that's the energy

00:30:08.520 --> 00:30:10.730
that really touches me

00:30:10.730 --> 00:30:13.050
and when we look at combining that energy

00:30:13.050 --> 00:30:14.537
with the energy of kind
of the young people

00:30:14.537 --> 00:30:15.870
and the people all over our country

00:30:15.870 --> 00:30:18.080
who are out in the
streets, I think we'll find

00:30:18.080 --> 00:30:20.040
that's the same energy
that created this country.

00:30:20.040 --> 00:30:22.650
It's the same energy that ended slavery

00:30:22.650 --> 00:30:24.660
and that established civil rights,

00:30:24.660 --> 00:30:26.000
and it's the same energy that I think

00:30:26.000 --> 00:30:28.350
is gonna carry us through
to the next chapter.

00:30:28.350 --> 00:30:30.480
On a slightly lighter
note, I want to point out

00:30:30.480 --> 00:30:32.720
your mother is the chair

00:30:32.720 --> 00:30:34.730
of the County Board of Commissioners.

00:30:34.730 --> 00:30:35.563
Yes.

00:30:35.563 --> 00:30:38.500
So the way the government's set up,

00:30:38.500 --> 00:30:42.230
and this really makes me smile,

00:30:42.230 --> 00:30:46.150
for you to get anything
done or approved as mayor,

00:30:46.150 --> 00:30:47.920
and a very influential mayor,

00:30:47.920 --> 00:30:49.930
you have to go to your mom.

00:30:49.930 --> 00:30:51.927
Well, not exactly, just because--

00:30:51.927 --> 00:30:54.400
(laughing)

00:30:54.400 --> 00:30:57.120
Conan, Conan, when I get in trouble

00:30:57.120 --> 00:30:59.130
and when I do stuff
that people don't like,

00:30:59.130 --> 00:31:00.450
they still go and get my mom.

00:31:00.450 --> 00:31:02.065
They're like should we bring Mrs. Carter

00:31:02.065 --> 00:31:03.940
into the conversation?
(laughing)

00:31:03.940 --> 00:31:06.140
So I have a city council that I work with,

00:31:06.140 --> 00:31:10.244
and we are the county
seat of Ramsey County,

00:31:10.244 --> 00:31:13.590
and so we work very closely
with the county board.

00:31:13.590 --> 00:31:17.160
I don't go to them to request

00:31:17.160 --> 00:31:19.210
direct approval of my budgets
or anything like that.

00:31:19.210 --> 00:31:21.710
I go to our city council, but we do

00:31:21.710 --> 00:31:23.650
work together very closely.

00:31:23.650 --> 00:31:25.990
I'm actually really proud
of the work that she's done,

00:31:25.990 --> 00:31:27.583
even in this space.

00:31:27.583 --> 00:31:31.300
She's led the process since
I think 2005 they started

00:31:31.300 --> 00:31:32.310
of an initiative here called

00:31:32.310 --> 00:31:34.610
Juvenile Detention
Alternatives Initiative,

00:31:34.610 --> 00:31:36.300
which started off just saying look,

00:31:36.300 --> 00:31:38.160
we're locking up too many young people.

00:31:38.160 --> 00:31:40.610
We had a time where we would go through

00:31:40.610 --> 00:31:42.250
our juvenile detention centers

00:31:42.250 --> 00:31:44.983
and it was so packed
that there would be cots

00:31:44.983 --> 00:31:48.130
in the hallways, 'cause
it'd be beyond capacity,

00:31:48.130 --> 00:31:50.250
and they started investing money.

00:31:50.250 --> 00:31:51.740
They started with a
million dollar investments

00:31:51.740 --> 00:31:54.150
into alternatives to detention
and just figuring out

00:31:54.150 --> 00:31:55.690
can we create evening reporting centers?

00:31:55.690 --> 00:31:59.570
Can we send young people
home on supervised release?

00:31:59.570 --> 00:32:01.880
What are all the different
things that we can do

00:32:01.880 --> 00:32:03.860
instead of bringing young people

00:32:03.860 --> 00:32:05.540
into the juvenile justice system?

00:32:05.540 --> 00:32:09.350
It's so important because
this is an incredible fact,

00:32:09.350 --> 00:32:11.170
but research shows that

00:32:12.790 --> 00:32:14.630
controlling for other factors,

00:32:14.630 --> 00:32:17.970
a young person who has spent
a night in juvenile detention

00:32:17.970 --> 00:32:20.690
is actually more likely
to be an adult offender.

00:32:20.690 --> 00:32:23.400
Our system is actually
making them more likely

00:32:23.400 --> 00:32:25.850
to be offenders in the
future, not less likely.

00:32:25.850 --> 00:32:28.540
That should be a gut check moment for us.

00:32:28.540 --> 00:32:30.950
All that to say is they have,

00:32:30.950 --> 00:32:34.140
through this Juvenile Detention
Alternatives Initiative,

00:32:34.140 --> 00:32:37.830
we have reduced average daily intake

00:32:37.830 --> 00:32:39.610
of young people in our community

00:32:39.610 --> 00:32:42.000
by I think over 80%.

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:45.130
They have shut down entire wings

00:32:45.130 --> 00:32:47.120
of that same juvenile detention center.

00:32:47.120 --> 00:32:50.180
Just last year we closed a
place called Boys Totem Town,

00:32:50.180 --> 00:32:53.380
which was a secure confinement facility

00:32:53.380 --> 00:32:54.870
for young people in our community,

00:32:54.870 --> 00:32:56.600
and we've done all of those things, Conan,

00:32:56.600 --> 00:33:00.310
without seeing any corresponding increase

00:33:00.310 --> 00:33:02.200
in juvenile crime in our community,

00:33:02.200 --> 00:33:04.097
and it goes to show that

00:33:04.097 --> 00:33:07.200
we've way over-utilized detention.

00:33:07.200 --> 00:33:09.180
We've way over-utilized confinement,

00:33:09.180 --> 00:33:11.450
and so we're excited for the chance

00:33:11.450 --> 00:33:15.070
to work together on kind of
pushing this vision forward.

00:33:15.070 --> 00:33:19.430
Well, I have to say what
an impressive family

00:33:19.430 --> 00:33:22.570
you come from, your mom, your dad,

00:33:22.570 --> 00:33:24.013
obviously yourself.

00:33:24.930 --> 00:33:27.020
You guys give me a lot of hope.

00:33:27.020 --> 00:33:29.160
I know that everybody
has to do the work now.

00:33:29.160 --> 00:33:32.410
I had a conversation with
W. Kamau Bell last week,

00:33:32.410 --> 00:33:34.110
and we talked about it's about the work

00:33:34.110 --> 00:33:35.250
and it's about doing the work,

00:33:35.250 --> 00:33:38.050
and that's not just you, that's not

00:33:38.050 --> 00:33:40.530
just people in the black
community, it's everybody,

00:33:40.530 --> 00:33:42.237
especially everybody.

00:33:43.964 --> 00:33:44.797
Yes.
Everybody has to

00:33:44.797 --> 00:33:48.620
do the work, but you
give me a lot of hope,

00:33:48.620 --> 00:33:52.270
you and your very impressive family

00:33:52.270 --> 00:33:53.470
give me a lot of hope,

00:33:53.470 --> 00:33:56.740
and there's a lot of ways that I probably,

00:33:56.740 --> 00:33:59.110
I don't know what it's like to be you,

00:33:59.110 --> 00:34:01.100
but what I do know is I
do know what it's like

00:34:01.100 --> 00:34:03.080
to have a three-month-old

00:34:03.080 --> 00:34:05.710
and you have my sympathy.

00:34:05.710 --> 00:34:07.719
I appreciate it, I appreciate it.

00:34:07.719 --> 00:34:09.390
She's great.

00:34:09.390 --> 00:34:10.720
You know, kidding aside,

00:34:10.720 --> 00:34:12.780
she's fantastic 24 hours a day,

00:34:12.780 --> 00:34:14.740
and I appreciate your
compliments of our family.

00:34:14.740 --> 00:34:15.770
I didn't get a chance to tell you

00:34:15.770 --> 00:34:17.620
about my sisters and my brother-in-laws,

00:34:17.620 --> 00:34:19.360
and my niece who just
graduated from high school,

00:34:19.360 --> 00:34:21.870
and my wife, who's a mid-wife,

00:34:21.870 --> 00:34:23.400
whose birthday it is today, so--

00:34:23.400 --> 00:34:24.690
Oh, happy birthday.
Happy birthday, sweetheart.

00:34:24.690 --> 00:34:25.523
Yeah.
Thank you,

00:34:25.523 --> 00:34:26.450
I appreciate that.

00:34:26.450 --> 00:34:27.763
I'll tell her you said so.

00:34:28.790 --> 00:34:30.297
I'm sure that'll mean the world to her.

00:34:30.297 --> 00:34:31.446
(laughing)
It will.

00:34:31.446 --> 00:34:32.937
It absolutely will.

00:34:32.937 --> 00:34:36.530
So yeah, we're surrounded
by a group of good folks,

00:34:36.530 --> 00:34:38.460
and you know, between our staff

00:34:38.460 --> 00:34:41.260
and the city workers and the
police and the firefighters

00:34:41.260 --> 00:34:43.010
who just served our city with distinction

00:34:43.010 --> 00:34:45.000
through these past couple of weeks,

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:47.190
I feel really blessed to be around

00:34:47.190 --> 00:34:49.250
just a pretty incredible crew of folks.

00:34:49.250 --> 00:34:52.660
Yeah, well, continued good luck to you

00:34:52.660 --> 00:34:54.050
in everything that you're doing,

00:34:54.050 --> 00:34:57.120
and if things get too rough

00:34:57.120 --> 00:34:59.130
with the three-month-old,
I'll say it again.

00:34:59.130 --> 00:35:00.570
You come stay at my place

00:35:00.570 --> 00:35:02.360
until things blow over.
Absolutely.

00:35:02.360 --> 00:35:04.770
Absolutely, if you could
just text me the directions,

00:35:04.770 --> 00:35:05.928
I'll be over.
(laughing)

00:35:05.928 --> 00:35:08.730
What you do is you tell the wife

00:35:08.730 --> 00:35:10.340
I've got some business I have to do.

00:35:10.340 --> 00:35:11.769
(laughing)
That's right.

00:35:11.769 --> 00:35:15.140
I'll be gone until she's
about four years old

00:35:15.140 --> 00:35:16.940
and then I'll come back.
Right.

00:35:16.940 --> 00:35:18.247
Right, oh it'll just be a few months.

00:35:18.247 --> 00:35:19.080
It'll just be a few
months, I'll be right back.

00:35:19.080 --> 00:35:20.860
I've got some things, yeah.

00:35:20.860 --> 00:35:24.130
And then hey, thank you so
much for taking the time.

00:35:24.130 --> 00:35:26.470
I really appreciate it, and I look forward

00:35:26.470 --> 00:35:28.390
